---
description: "Strict, mandatory rules for code style"
alwaysApply: true
---

Every rule here is mandatory. On conflict, the higher item wins:
1. An explicit instruction from the user in the current conversation.
2. The existing codebase's architecture, style, and patterns.
3. A more specific rule over a more general one.

# Communication
- Reply in the user's language with concise, direct prose: full sentences, no jargon, slang, or shorthand.
- Push back on technical mistakes; defer to the user on vision and architecture.

# Workflow
- Fix the root cause, not the symptom. When the root cause is upstream, draft an issue and wait; do not work around it.
- For any non-trivial change, propose at least three solutions with trade-offs and ask which to take before implementing.
- Never work around a design obstacle; stop and ask.
- Stay on scope: minimal, focused changes. Report smells and needed refactoring as issue drafts instead of fixing them.
- Mark deliberately unfinished work with a puzzle: `// @todo #<issue> <what remains>`.
- Fix checker violations in the code you touch; never suppress a checker.
- Use the UI library the project already has; do not build components it already provides.
- When a problem stays hard, add diagnostic logging to production code.

# Code
- Among working solutions, choose the most elegant one.
- Comments explain why, never what. Each class starts with a plain-text docblock stating its purpose, not its usage.
- Variables are single nouns. Methods are queries named as nouns or commands named as verbs, never both. Tests are exempt.
- Inline single-use expressions; drop needless locals.
- No blank lines inside method bodies; at most one between blocks.
- Error and log messages: one sentence with context, no trailing period.
- Paired brackets: a bracket closed on another line closes at the start of that line, at the opening line's indentation.
- Monotonic indentation: each line indents at most one level deeper than the previous.

# Objects (OOP languages)
- Follow Elegant Objects, SOLID, DDD, YAGNI, DRY; where they disagree, Elegant Objects wins.
- Fail fast; never fail silently.
- Compose; never inherit implementation.
- One primary constructor with assignments only; secondary constructors delegate to it.
- Every class is final and immutable, with one to four attributes.
- No -er names, utility classes, static methods, public static constants, getters, or setters.
- Every public method is declared in an interface first; nothing else is public except constructors.
- Never pass or return null; convert nulls from external APIs at the boundary.
- No type introspection, casting, or reflection.
- Exception: constructs the language or framework requires (`main`, `equals`, JUnit static sources, test classes) — keep them minimal.
